<BaseLayout
  title="A neural-network decoder for surface-code error correction"
  description="Why a quantum computer can detect an error without learning what it was, and an experimental study of how neural decoders behave as architecture, training scale, code configuration and hardware constraints change."
>
  <div class="shell">
    <article>
      <header class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory · 2026</p>
        <h1 class="measure">A neural-network decoder for surface-code error correction</h1>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Summary</h2>

        {/*
          Deliberately small and floated rather than a hero image. The page is
          a research story; a full-width photograph of people would make it
          read as a profile instead. It sits in the summary because that is
          where a reader has just learned what the project is, and where
          saying who it is done with adds context rather than interrupting.
        */}
        <figure class="photo-inset">
          <img
            src="/work/qec/advisors.jpg"
            alt="Three people standing together in a laboratory office corridor, all wearing Fermilab lanyards."
            width="1200"
            height="897"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>
            With my research advisors at Fermilab: Dr. Gabriel Perdue, Senior
            Scientist, and Dr. Giuseppe Di Guglielmo, Principal Engineer.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          A quantum computer cannot look at its own state to see whether it has
          been damaged — looking destroys it. Instead it measures a set of
          carefully chosen parities that reveal that something went wrong while
          revealing nothing about the encoded information. The catch is that
          those measurements are also nearly silent about the error itself:
          many different physical errors produce exactly the same measurement
          outcome, and a decoder has to choose between them, correctly, in
          about a microsecond, forever.
        </p>
        <p>
          The standard answer is minimum-weight perfect matching, which assumes
          the simplest explanation is the right one. It is fast, it is
          principled, and it is provably not optimal. Neural decoders can
          recover some of what matching throws away. This project is an
          experimental study of when they do: how the advantage responds to
          model architecture, to how much data the model has seen, to the size
          of the code being protected, and to the severe constraints of
          hardware that has to keep pace with the machine it is correcting.
        </p>

        <h2>The research question</h2>
        <p>
          Matching picks the single most likely error <em>chain</em>. What
          determines whether the logical qubit survives is the most likely
          error <em>class</em> — the whole family of chains that would need the
          same correction. Those two are not the same, and the gap between them
          is where an optimal decoder lives.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the question is not simply whether a network can beat matching.
          It is: <strong>how much of the structure matching misses do neural
          decoders actually capture, and what happens to that as the
          architecture, the training scale, the code configuration and the
          hardware budget change?</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Those four turn out to pull against each other. A model large enough
          to learn the structure may be too large to run inside the correction
          cycle; an advantage measured on a small code at one noise strength
          may not survive at a larger one. The useful result is not a single
          number but the shape of those trade-offs.
        </p>

        <figure class="square-figure">
          <img
            src={FIGURES[0].src}
            alt={FIGURES[0].alt}
            width={FIGURES[0].width}
            height={FIGURES[0].height}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>{FIGURES[0].caption}</figcaption>
        </figure>

        {/*
          The interactive lattice sits here rather than on the home page: this
          is the one place on the site where a visitor already has the context
          to know what they are looking at, and it makes the ambiguity above
          something they can produce themselves rather than take on trust.
        */}
        <h3>Try it</h3>
        <p>
          Put a few errors in a line and watch the interior checks fall silent.
          A chain of length one and a chain of length five can leave the same
          two checks lit — that is the ambiguity the decoder has to resolve, and
          it is not a limitation of the measurement apparatus but of what a
          parity check is.
        </p>

        <SurfaceCode />

        <h2>Method</h2>
        <h3>Data</h3>
        <p>
          Syndromes were generated with circuit-level noise rather than a
          simplified independent-error model — the measurement apparatus is
          itself faulty, so the syndrome arrives as a noisy time series over
          repeated rounds and not as one clean snapshot. Training used tens of
          millions of shots; the model is only converged at that scale, and
          conclusions drawn from smaller runs were, repeatedly, artifacts.
        </p>

        <figure class="square-figure">
          <img
            src={FIGURES[1].src}
            alt={FIGURES[1].alt}
            width={FIGURES[1].width}
            height={FIGURES[1].height}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>{FIGURES[1].caption}</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>Model</h3>
        <p>
          This project builds on a recurrent–convolutional (RCNN) decoder
          developed by Ulaşcan. The architecture combines a recurrent stage
          that processes syndrome rounds over time with a convolutional stage
          that exploits the spatial structure of the surface-code lattice. It
          predicts one bit — whether a logical flip has occurred — because that
          is the only question with a well-defined answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          My work focuses on experimentally evaluating that decoder: how its
          performance scales with training data and code configuration, how it
          compares with simpler MLP and GRU architectures, and how much it can
          be compressed for eventual FPGA deployment.
        </p>

        <figure>
          <img
            src={FIGURES[2].src}
            alt={FIGURES[2].alt}
            width={FIGURES[2].width}
            height={FIGURES[2].height}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>{FIGURES[2].caption}</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>Evaluation</h3>
        <p>
          Against matching on an identical held-out set of shots, with paired
          statistics rather than two independently reported numbers — the
          comparison of interest is per-shot agreement and disagreement, which
          a pair of aggregate error rates throws away.
        </p>
        <p>
          Run-to-run variation turned out to be large enough to swamp the
          effect being measured, so every reported number is a mean over
          independent training runs with a band, and single-run results are not
          treated as evidence.
        </p>

        <h2>Results</h2>

        {/*
          The animation does not autoplay. An animated GIF cannot be paused by
          the reader and ignores prefers-reduced-motion entirely — there is no
          CSS or markup that stops it. Showing the final frame and letting the
          reader ask for the animation is the only way to respect that setting
          with this format, and it also spares everyone a 1.2 MB download they
          did not ask for.
        */}
        <figure class="anim-figure">
          <img
            data-anim-image
            data-still={FIGURES[3].src}
            data-animated={FIGURES[3].animated}
            src={FIGURES[3].src}
            alt={FIGURES[3].alt}
            width={FIGURES[3].width}
            height={FIGURES[3].height}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <div class="anim-figure__bar">
            {/* Hidden until the script runs: without JavaScript the button
                would do nothing, and a dead control is worse than none. */}
            <button type="button" class="anim-figure__button" data-anim-toggle hidden>
              Play training animation
            </button>
          </div>
          <figcaption>{FIGURES[3].caption}</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Every training run to date, at each code distance. The column that
          matters is <strong>×MWPM</strong> — the logical error rate as a
          multiple of what minimum-weight matching achieves on the same shots.
          Below 1.00 the decoder is doing better than matching; above it, worse.
          Where a run was repeated across seeds the spread is given, because
          run-to-run variation on this task is large enough to be mistaken for
          a result.
        </p>

        {/* The rehype plugin that wraps tables only runs over Markdown, so a
            table written directly in a .astro page needs the scroll container
            added by hand. This one is thirteen columns wide and will scroll
            sideways on almost any screen — that is intended. Dropping columns
            to make it fit would make it a summary rather than a record. */}
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="runs">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col" class="num">d</th>
                <th scope="col">Model</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">Params</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">Batch</th>
                <th scope="col">Shots</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">Seeds</th>
                <th scope="col">p<sub>L</sub></th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">MWPM p<sub>L</sub></th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">×MWPM</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">s/epoch</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">Total (1 seed)</th>
                <th scope="col">Hardware</th>
                <th scope="col">Completed</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {RUNS.map((run) => (
                <tr class={run.highlight ? 'is-headline' : undefined}>
                  <td class="num">{run.d}</td>
                  <td>
                    {run.model}
                    {run.footnote && (
                      <sup class="fn-marker">{run.footnote}</sup>
                    )}
                  </td>
                  <td class="num">{run.params}</td>
                  <td class="num">{run.batch}</td>
                  <td>{run.shots}</td>
                  <td class="num">{run.seeds}</td>
                  <td class="num">{run.pL}</td>
                  <td class="num">{run.mwpm}</td>
                  <td class="num">{run.ratio}</td>
                  <td class="num">{run.perEpoch}</td>
                  <td class="num">{run.total}</td>
                  <td>{run.hardware}</td>
                  <td>{run.date}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <ol class="footnotes meta">
          {FOOTNOTES.map((note) => <li>{note}</li>)}
        </ol>

        <figure>
          <img
            src={FIGURES[4].src}
            alt={FIGURES[4].alt}
            width={FIGURES[4].width}
            height={FIGURES[4].height}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>{FIGURES[4].caption}</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The failure analysis was the part that changed how I thought about
          the problem. Errors are not uniformly distributed across the lattice,
          and looking at the actual circuit at the moment a shot fails is far
          more informative than another point on an error-rate curve.
        </p>

        <h2>Current status</h2>
        <p>
          Ongoing. I am studying the trade-offs among decoder accuracy, model
          architecture, parameter count and deployment cost. That includes
          comparisons among RCNN, GRU and MLP models, model compression and
          quantization, and tests of how performance changes as the
          surface-code distance and the number of syndrome rounds increase.
        </p>
        <p>
          The constraint underneath all of it: a decoder that runs in the
          control loop of a real machine has to fit on an FPGA and finish
          inside the error-correction cycle. Accuracy that arrives too late is
          not accuracy.
        </p>

        <h2>Links</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="https://github.com/karriechey/FNAL-QCDecoding-FPGA" rel="noopener">
              Code — FNAL-QCDecoding-FPGA
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/karrie-oqi-symposium-slides.pdf">Slides — OQI Symposium</a>
          </li>
          <li><a href="/guides/parity-checks-and-syndromes/">Background: what a syndrome does and does not tell you</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="meta back-link">
        <a href="/work">Back to work</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  /*
   * Opt-in playback for the training animation.
   *
   * Swaps the still frame for the GIF and back. Restarting a GIF from its
   * first frame is not possible through the DOM — the browser caches it and
   * resumes mid-loop — so each play appends a cache-busting query string,
   * which forces a fresh decode from frame one.
   *
   * The button starts hidden in the markup and is revealed here, so a reader
   * without JavaScript sees the still figure and no dead control.
   */
  const image = document.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>('[data-anim-image]');
  const toggle = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-anim-toggle]');

  if (image && toggle) {
    const still = image.dataset.still ?? '';
    const animated = image.dataset.animated ?? '';
    let playing = false;

    toggle.hidden = false;

    toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
      playing = !playing;
      image.src = playing ? `${animated}?t=${Date.now()}` : still;
      toggle.textContent = playing ? 'Show final frame' : 'Play training animation';
    });
  }
</script>

